1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,590
Twelve hundred dollars.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:04,521
That is what the state of Alaska

3
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:07,136
paid to almost every resident in twenty

4
00:00:07,136 --> 00:00:08,340
twenty-six.

5
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:08,932
Men,

6
00:00:08,932 --> 00:00:09,760
women,

7
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:10,588
children,

8
00:00:10,588 --> 00:00:12,330
even babies.

9
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:14,140
No work required.

10
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:16,673
Just live there for the whole year.

11
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:19,872
But by the formula written in Alaska's

12
00:00:19,872 --> 00:00:20,587
own law,

13
00:00:20,587 --> 00:00:23,057
the check should have been much bigger.

14
00:00:23,057 --> 00:00:25,151
The governor's office put the full amount

15
00:00:25,151 --> 00:00:27,055
at three thousand six hundred and fifty

16
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:28,607
dollars per person.

17
00:00:28,627 --> 00:00:30,602
The legislature paid about a third of

18
00:00:30,602 --> 00:00:31,381
that.

19
00:00:31,617 --> 00:00:34,277
So where does this money come from?

20
00:00:34,277 --> 00:00:36,884
And why does the check keep shrinking?

21
00:00:37,117 --> 00:00:39,106
By the end of this video,

22
00:00:39,106 --> 00:00:41,372
you will know how oil money became

23
00:00:41,372 --> 00:00:43,865
a ninety billion dollar fund,

24
00:00:43,865 --> 00:00:45,854
how the dividend is paid,

25
00:00:45,854 --> 00:00:48,095
why the state now depends on it,

26
00:00:48,095 --> 00:00:50,361
and what economists found when they

27
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:51,969
studied the checks.

28
00:00:57,617 --> 00:00:59,912
The first check went out in nineteen

29
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:01,167
eighty-two.

30
00:01:01,167 --> 00:01:03,092
It was one thousand dollars for every

31
00:01:03,092 --> 00:01:04,887
eligible resident.

32
00:01:04,887 --> 00:01:05,740
In total,

33
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:07,708
the state paid out about four hundred

34
00:01:07,708 --> 00:01:10,495
and seventy-one million dollars that year.

35
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:13,000
The rules are simple.

36
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:14,947
You must live in Alaska for the

37
00:01:14,947 --> 00:01:16,417
whole calendar year,

38
00:01:16,417 --> 00:01:19,360
and plan to stay there indefinitely.

39
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:21,339
You must also have spent at least

40
00:01:21,339 --> 00:01:23,675
seventy-two hours in a row in the

41
00:01:23,675 --> 00:01:25,910
state during the past two years.

42
00:01:26,110 --> 00:01:27,755
In twenty twenty-five,

43
00:01:27,755 --> 00:01:30,377
six hundred and sixty-five thousand people

44
00:01:30,377 --> 00:01:31,068
applied,

45
00:01:31,068 --> 00:01:33,452
and six hundred and eighteen thousand were

46
00:01:33,452 --> 00:01:34,290
paid.

47
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:35,902
That is most of a state with

48
00:01:35,902 --> 00:01:37,904
about seven hundred and thirty-nine

49
00:01:37,904 --> 00:01:39,305
thousand people.

50
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:42,450
The amount changes every year.

51
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:43,638
In twenty fifteen,

52
00:01:43,638 --> 00:01:45,652
the dividend reached two thousand and

53
00:01:45,652 --> 00:01:47,460
seventy-two dollars.

54
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,360
The largest payment ever,

55
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,352
in dollar terms,

56
00:01:50,352 --> 00:01:52,350
came in twenty twenty-two.

57
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:54,758
With an extra energy relief payment,

58
00:01:54,758 --> 00:01:56,578
it was three thousand two hundred and

59
00:01:56,578 --> 00:01:58,190
eighty-four dollars.

60
00:01:58,580 --> 00:01:59,971
In twenty twenty-four,

61
00:01:59,971 --> 00:02:01,987
it was one thousand seven hundred and

62
00:02:01,987 --> 00:02:02,774
two dollars,

63
00:02:02,774 --> 00:02:05,390
again including energy relief.

64
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,064
In twenty twenty-five,

65
00:02:07,064 --> 00:02:08,878
it was back down to one thousand

66
00:02:08,878 --> 00:02:09,932
dollars.

67
00:02:10,183 --> 00:02:11,593
Add it all up.

68
00:02:11,593 --> 00:02:13,954
From nineteen eighty-two to twenty

69
00:02:13,954 --> 00:02:14,874
twenty-five,

70
00:02:14,874 --> 00:02:16,999
the state paid out about thirty-one point

71
00:02:16,999 --> 00:02:19,723
three billion dollars in dividends.

72
00:02:19,913 --> 00:02:22,399
Someone who collected every single check

73
00:02:22,399 --> 00:02:24,471
would have received about fifty-two

74
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:26,107
thousand eight hundred dollars,

75
00:02:26,107 --> 00:02:27,743
before inflation.

76
00:02:27,983 --> 00:02:31,103
Let me draw the checks over time.

77
00:02:31,303 --> 00:02:33,538
Here is nineteen eighty-two,

78
00:02:33,538 --> 00:02:35,393
one thousand dollars.

79
00:02:35,563 --> 00:02:37,507
The bars rise and fall with the

80
00:02:37,507 --> 00:02:38,663
markets.

81
00:02:38,773 --> 00:02:41,133
A high point in twenty fifteen.

82
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:43,473
A cut in twenty sixteen.

83
00:02:43,483 --> 00:02:45,943
A record in twenty twenty-two.

84
00:02:46,013 --> 00:02:47,091
And today,

85
00:02:47,091 --> 00:02:49,073
twelve hundred dollars.

86
00:02:49,143 --> 00:02:51,125
The check is a roller coaster,

87
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:52,838
not a salary.

88
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:55,658
To understand why the check moves so

89
00:02:55,658 --> 00:02:56,147
much,

90
00:02:56,147 --> 00:02:58,465
follow it back to where the money

91
00:02:58,465 --> 00:02:59,580
is kept.

92
00:03:10,217 --> 00:03:12,346
In nineteen seventy-six,

93
00:03:12,346 --> 00:03:14,476
as oil money began to flow,

94
00:03:14,476 --> 00:03:16,390
Alaskans voted to change their

95
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:17,947
constitution.

96
00:03:18,047 --> 00:03:19,884
At least twenty-five percent of the

97
00:03:19,884 --> 00:03:22,128
state's oil and mineral royalties would go

98
00:03:22,128 --> 00:03:24,157
into a permanent fund.

99
00:03:24,397 --> 00:03:25,068
Later,

100
00:03:25,068 --> 00:03:27,152
a state law raised that to fifty

101
00:03:27,152 --> 00:03:29,372
percent for newer leases.

102
00:03:29,617 --> 00:03:31,827
The fund has two rooms.

103
00:03:31,827 --> 00:03:33,627
The principal is locked.

104
00:03:33,627 --> 00:03:36,627
The constitution says it cannot be spent.

105
00:03:36,687 --> 00:03:37,506
The earnings,

106
00:03:37,506 --> 00:03:39,376
the profits from investing it,

107
00:03:39,376 --> 00:03:41,246
go into a second account,

108
00:03:41,246 --> 00:03:43,317
called the earnings reserve.

109
00:03:43,577 --> 00:03:45,531
That second room is where the dividend

110
00:03:45,531 --> 00:03:46,717
comes from.

111
00:03:46,847 --> 00:03:49,448
At the end of June twenty twenty-six,

112
00:03:49,448 --> 00:03:52,073
the fund's managers reported about ten

113
00:03:52,073 --> 00:03:54,459
point six billion dollars of realized

114
00:03:54,459 --> 00:03:55,867
earnings in that room,

115
00:03:55,867 --> 00:03:58,492
plus four billion already set aside for

116
00:03:58,492 --> 00:04:00,047
the next year's draw.

117
00:04:00,217 --> 00:04:01,944
Unlike the principal,

118
00:04:01,944 --> 00:04:04,196
the legislature can spend this room

119
00:04:04,196 --> 00:04:05,923
through the yearly budget,

120
00:04:05,923 --> 00:04:08,426
as the HB two sixty-three budget did

121
00:04:08,426 --> 00:04:09,527
this year.

122
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:12,553
Today the fund is huge.

123
00:04:12,553 --> 00:04:15,057
At the end of June twenty twenty-six,

124
00:04:15,057 --> 00:04:17,354
it was worth about ninety-one point nine

125
00:04:17,354 --> 00:04:18,873
billion dollars.

126
00:04:18,893 --> 00:04:19,491
Of that,

127
00:04:19,491 --> 00:04:21,554
about fifty-nine billion was locked

128
00:04:21,554 --> 00:04:22,773
principal.

129
00:04:22,913 --> 00:04:24,412
In the last fiscal year,

130
00:04:24,412 --> 00:04:26,367
it earned a return of more than

131
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:27,215
twelve percent,

132
00:04:27,215 --> 00:04:28,953
and the state drew about three point

133
00:04:28,953 --> 00:04:31,073
eight billion dollars from it.

134
00:04:31,317 --> 00:04:33,837
Here is the surprising part.

135
00:04:33,967 --> 00:04:36,217
The fund is no longer mainly an

136
00:04:36,217 --> 00:04:37,317
oil fund.

137
00:04:37,357 --> 00:04:39,845
Royalties added only about five hundred

138
00:04:39,845 --> 00:04:42,707
and thirty-five million dollars last year.

139
00:04:42,827 --> 00:04:44,994
Most of its value now comes from

140
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:47,675
decades of investing in stocks,

141
00:04:47,675 --> 00:04:50,984
bonds and other assets around the world.

142
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:53,430
Let me draw the vault.

143
00:04:53,430 --> 00:04:55,077
Here is the pipeline,

144
00:04:55,077 --> 00:04:57,000
bringing in oil royalties.

145
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,773
They flow into the locked room,

146
00:04:58,773 --> 00:05:00,160
the principal.

147
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:01,433
The investments grow,

148
00:05:01,433 --> 00:05:03,591
and the profits spill into the second

149
00:05:03,591 --> 00:05:04,001
room,

150
00:05:04,001 --> 00:05:05,670
the earnings reserve.

151
00:05:05,670 --> 00:05:07,174
And from that second room,

152
00:05:07,174 --> 00:05:08,679
the state takes its draw,

153
00:05:08,679 --> 00:05:10,587
and pays the dividend.

154
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:11,549
For years,

155
00:05:11,549 --> 00:05:14,560
the dividend had its own formula.

156
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:17,185
Then the state ran short of money.

157
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:24,295
In twenty sixteen,

158
00:05:24,295 --> 00:05:26,745
oil prices had collapsed and the state

159
00:05:26,745 --> 00:05:29,060
budget was in deep trouble.

160
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:30,872
The governor at the time,

161
00:05:30,872 --> 00:05:31,732
Bill Walker,

162
00:05:31,732 --> 00:05:33,716
cut the money set aside for the

163
00:05:33,716 --> 00:05:35,860
dividend roughly in half.

164
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:37,927
The check that year was one thousand

165
00:05:37,927 --> 00:05:40,002
and twenty-two dollars.

166
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:41,835
A lawsuit followed,

167
00:05:41,835 --> 00:05:44,590
arguing that the dividend was protected by

168
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:45,420
law.

169
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:47,230
In twenty seventeen,

170
00:05:47,230 --> 00:05:50,280
the Alaska Supreme Court disagreed.

171
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,350
Its words were clear.

172
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:55,520
Without another constitutional amendment,

173
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:57,728
the dividend must compete for funding

174
00:05:57,728 --> 00:05:58,811
every year,

175
00:05:58,811 --> 00:06:01,398
just like any other state program.

176
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:02,930
In twenty eighteen,

177
00:06:02,930 --> 00:06:05,700
the legislature set a new rule.

178
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:06,595
Each year,

179
00:06:06,595 --> 00:06:08,677
the state may draw five percent of

180
00:06:08,677 --> 00:06:11,020
the fund's average value from recent

181
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:11,880
years.

182
00:06:12,050 --> 00:06:13,765
The idea was a steady,

183
00:06:13,765 --> 00:06:16,152
predictable payout instead of a new fight

184
00:06:16,152 --> 00:06:17,620
every year.

185
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:20,294
That single draw now pays for two

186
00:06:20,294 --> 00:06:21,800
things at once.

187
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:22,876
The dividend,

188
00:06:22,876 --> 00:06:25,451
and the rest of the state government.

189
00:06:25,717 --> 00:06:28,507
And the government needs it badly.

190
00:06:28,517 --> 00:06:30,652
In fiscal year twenty twenty-five,

191
00:06:30,652 --> 00:06:32,809
by our calculation from the state's

192
00:06:32,809 --> 00:06:34,082
revenue forecast,

193
00:06:34,082 --> 00:06:36,023
the Permanent Fund supplied about

194
00:06:36,023 --> 00:06:38,396
fifty-eight percent of the state's general

195
00:06:38,396 --> 00:06:39,427
revenue.

196
00:06:39,617 --> 00:06:42,447
Oil supplied only about thirty percent.

197
00:06:42,617 --> 00:06:45,489
The fund has replaced oil as Alaska's

198
00:06:45,489 --> 00:06:47,134
biggest paycheck.

199
00:06:47,383 --> 00:06:50,053
Let me draw the tug of war.

200
00:06:50,153 --> 00:06:52,197
Here is the yearly draw from the

201
00:06:52,197 --> 00:06:52,682
fund,

202
00:06:52,682 --> 00:06:53,713
one pie.

203
00:06:53,713 --> 00:06:54,734
On one side,

204
00:06:54,734 --> 00:06:56,613
the dividend checks.

205
00:06:56,613 --> 00:06:58,179
On the other side,

206
00:06:58,179 --> 00:06:58,683
schools,

207
00:06:58,683 --> 00:07:01,338
roads and everything else the state pays

208
00:07:01,338 --> 00:07:02,203
for.

209
00:07:02,303 --> 00:07:04,535
Every dollar that goes to one side

210
00:07:04,535 --> 00:07:06,698
cannot go to the other.

211
00:07:06,950 --> 00:07:09,390
Which is exactly the fight that played

212
00:07:09,390 --> 00:07:12,429
out again in twenty twenty-six.

213
00:07:18,083 --> 00:07:20,529
The governor's budget proposed a full

214
00:07:20,529 --> 00:07:22,953
dividend under the old formula,

215
00:07:22,953 --> 00:07:25,399
about three thousand six hundred and fifty

216
00:07:25,399 --> 00:07:27,333
dollars per Alaskan.

217
00:07:27,543 --> 00:07:29,991
The legislature chose something much

218
00:07:29,991 --> 00:07:31,080
smaller.

219
00:07:31,317 --> 00:07:33,520
The final budget paid a dividend of

220
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:34,701
one thousand dollars,

221
00:07:34,701 --> 00:07:36,904
estimated at about six hundred and

222
00:07:36,904 --> 00:07:39,707
seventy-four million dollars in total.

223
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:42,104
It added a two hundred dollar energy

224
00:07:42,104 --> 00:07:43,017
relief payment,

225
00:07:43,017 --> 00:07:44,881
but only if the state's revenue came

226
00:07:44,881 --> 00:07:46,227
in high enough.

227
00:07:46,317 --> 00:07:47,044
It did,

228
00:07:47,044 --> 00:07:49,298
and the final check was twelve hundred

229
00:07:49,298 --> 00:07:50,399
dollars.

230
00:07:50,650 --> 00:07:52,480
To pay for all of it,

231
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:55,133
the same budget drew about four billion

232
00:07:55,133 --> 00:07:58,120
dollars from the fund's earnings reserve.

233
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:00,063
By our calculation,

234
00:08:00,063 --> 00:08:02,257
most of that money went to running

235
00:08:02,257 --> 00:08:02,965
the state,

236
00:08:02,965 --> 00:08:04,540
not to the checks.

237
00:08:04,790 --> 00:08:05,921
The dividend,

238
00:08:05,921 --> 00:08:07,053
in other words,

239
00:08:07,053 --> 00:08:08,900
is now the smaller part of the

240
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:10,654
fund's yearly paycheck.

241
00:08:10,917 --> 00:08:14,067
The fund itself felt the squeeze too.

242
00:08:14,187 --> 00:08:14,988
Normally,

243
00:08:14,988 --> 00:08:17,042
part of the earnings is moved into

244
00:08:17,042 --> 00:08:18,891
the principal to protect it from

245
00:08:18,891 --> 00:08:20,107
inflation.

246
00:08:20,197 --> 00:08:20,884
This year,

247
00:08:20,884 --> 00:08:23,228
according to the fund's managers,

248
00:08:23,228 --> 00:08:25,123
that one point five billion dollar

249
00:08:25,123 --> 00:08:26,757
transfer did not happen,

250
00:08:26,757 --> 00:08:29,362
because the legislature did not approve

251
00:08:29,362 --> 00:08:30,198
it.

252
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:32,980
Let me draw the ghost check.

253
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:34,731
Here is the real check,

254
00:08:34,731 --> 00:08:36,680
twelve hundred dollars.

255
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:37,669
Above it,

256
00:08:37,669 --> 00:08:38,102
faint,

257
00:08:38,102 --> 00:08:40,155
the check the old formula would have

258
00:08:40,155 --> 00:08:40,588
paid,

259
00:08:40,588 --> 00:08:43,240
three thousand six hundred and fifty.

260
00:08:43,330 --> 00:08:45,639
The gap between them is the money

261
00:08:45,639 --> 00:08:48,548
the state kept to run its government.

262
00:08:48,783 --> 00:08:51,050
So what does a yearly check actually

263
00:08:51,050 --> 00:08:53,463
do to the people who get it?

264
00:08:53,753 --> 00:08:56,687
Economists have studied that question for

265
00:08:56,687 --> 00:08:57,554
years.

266
00:09:03,217 --> 00:09:05,533
The big fear about free money is

267
00:09:05,533 --> 00:09:07,677
that people stop working.

268
00:09:07,787 --> 00:09:11,244
Economists Damon Jones and Ioana Marinescu

269
00:09:11,244 --> 00:09:13,457
tested that in Alaska.

270
00:09:13,707 --> 00:09:16,167
They found no effect on employment.

271
00:09:16,317 --> 00:09:18,940
Part-time work actually rose a little,

272
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:21,106
by about one point eight percentage

273
00:09:21,106 --> 00:09:21,947
points.

274
00:09:22,117 --> 00:09:23,835
Their study was published in a

275
00:09:23,835 --> 00:09:26,316
peer-reviewed economics journal in twenty

276
00:09:26,316 --> 00:09:27,489
twenty-two.

277
00:09:27,750 --> 00:09:29,990
People do spend it.

278
00:09:30,110 --> 00:09:32,488
Another study found that households spend

279
00:09:32,488 --> 00:09:34,650
about a quarter of the payment on

280
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:37,244
everyday goods and services within three

281
00:09:37,244 --> 00:09:38,060
months.

282
00:09:38,490 --> 00:09:40,995
Researchers have also linked an extra

283
00:09:40,995 --> 00:09:43,272
thousand dollars to slightly higher birth

284
00:09:43,272 --> 00:09:44,100
weights.

285
00:09:44,290 --> 00:09:46,317
And one study of crime found a

286
00:09:46,317 --> 00:09:49,104
jump in substance-abuse incidents the day

287
00:09:49,104 --> 00:09:50,853
after the checks arrive,

288
00:09:50,853 --> 00:09:52,601
about fourteen percent,

289
00:09:52,601 --> 00:09:55,135
while property crime fell about eight

290
00:09:55,135 --> 00:09:55,870
percent,

291
00:09:55,870 --> 00:09:58,243
and violent crime did not change.

292
00:09:58,483 --> 00:10:01,303
Let me draw what the research says.

293
00:10:01,353 --> 00:10:03,643
Here is the check arriving.

294
00:10:03,683 --> 00:10:04,830
One arrow,

295
00:10:04,830 --> 00:10:05,683
to jobs,

296
00:10:05,683 --> 00:10:07,753
with almost no change.

297
00:10:07,893 --> 00:10:08,743
One arrow,

298
00:10:08,743 --> 00:10:09,593
to spending,

299
00:10:09,593 --> 00:10:12,373
about a quarter within a few months.

300
00:10:12,643 --> 00:10:14,258
And a few small arrows,

301
00:10:14,258 --> 00:10:16,263
to health and safety.

302
00:10:16,593 --> 00:10:18,856
A cash payment that people use,

303
00:10:18,856 --> 00:10:21,491
without walking away from work.

304
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:24,065
So why does Alaska pay you to

305
00:10:24,065 --> 00:10:25,180
live there?

306
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,400
Because in nineteen seventy-six,

307
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:29,647
it decided that oil money belonged to

308
00:10:29,647 --> 00:10:30,377
everyone,

309
00:10:30,377 --> 00:10:32,100
and locked it away.

310
00:10:32,270 --> 00:10:35,184
That fund grew to about ninety-two billion

311
00:10:35,184 --> 00:10:36,270
dollars.

312
00:10:36,530 --> 00:10:38,265
But once the state started paying its

313
00:10:38,265 --> 00:10:39,761
bills from the same account,

314
00:10:39,761 --> 00:10:42,530
every dividend became a choice.

315
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:45,150
The check is not shrinking by accident.

316
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:47,120
It is shrinking by vote.

317
00:10:47,350 --> 00:10:48,294
Every spring,

318
00:10:48,294 --> 00:10:50,800
the legislature decides again how much of

319
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:52,515
the oil fortune goes to people,

320
00:10:52,515 --> 00:10:54,850
and how much goes to the government.

321
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:56,670
The fund keeps growing.

322
00:10:56,670 --> 00:10:59,170
The argument over the check does too.

323
00:10:59,417 --> 00:11:01,764
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

324
00:11:01,764 --> 00:11:04,564
we follow the money behind another hidden

325
00:11:04,564 --> 00:11:06,887
link of the world economy.

326
00:11:07,077 --> 00:11:09,206
This is The Hidden Economics.
